<figure><img sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" data-recalc-dims="1" height="778" width="1360" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Glenn Fleishman, art by Shafer Brown" /></figure>
<p>A lot of mythology swirls around the Coca-Cola Company&#8217;s strange set of decisions in 1985 to release a reformulated version of its historic carbonated beverage in April—the so-called &#8220;New Coke&#8221;—then, a few months later, start producing the original flavor as &#8220;Coke Classic.&#8221; Movies, books, and lengthy blog entries offer competing narratives about what actually happened.</p>
<figure class="pull-right narrow"><img height="1274" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) 23vw, 173px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/coke-classic-can-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/coke-classic-can-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/coke-classic-can-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/coke-classic-can-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/coke-classic-can-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="690" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/coke-classic-can-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="A red Coca-Cola Classic can covered in condensation droplets, featuring the white script logo and a curving white and yellow ribbon design." /><figcaption>Coke brings good things back from the dead.</figcaption></figure>
<p>In the end, Coke Classic won. People liked its taste better, and New Coke—renamed Coke II in 1990—disappeared entirely in 2002.<sup id="fnref-42133-confidence"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/siri-classic-brings-old-things-back-to-life/#fn-42133-confidence" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">1</a></sup></p>
<p>You might think this is an analogy for Siri AI and the original Siri, which Apple has sort of dubbed &#8220;Siri Classic.&#8221; I say &#8220;sort of&#8221; because you might not know Apple uses that name at all for the Siri-before-Siri AI.</p>
<p>And I don&#8217;t think Siri Classic has the great taste we all missed with the release of Siri AI. But I&#8217;m getting ahead of myself.</p>
<p>This column is really about how you can turn off Siri AI and some Apple Intelligence features in the version 27 operating systems, even though you can&#8217;t <em>per se</em> disable Apple Intelligence altogether.</p>
<h2>Hidden uncanny valley settings</h2>
<p>When Apple introduced Apple Intelligence, it appeared in Siri &amp; Apple Intelligence in Settings and System Settings. If you so chose, you could disable Apple Intelligence and leave everything else on.</p>
<p>The release of iOS 27, iPadOS 27, and macOS 27 omitted that switch. You can still turn Siri on or off altogether, and some clever folks realized you could <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/127893">set Siri&#8217;s language to one not supported by Siri AI</a> and Apple Intelligence<sup id="fnref-42133-language"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/siri-classic-brings-old-things-back-to-life/#fn-42133-language" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">2</a></sup> to disable it, although that choice can cause other inconveniences.</p>

<p>If that&#8217;s not to your liking, you can flip a variety of Apple Intelligence features off using Screen Time. This isn&#8217;t a hidden feature, but it&#8217;s not advertised—you can&#8217;t find it in Settings/System Settings: Siri, for instance.</p>
<p>Take a deep breath and go to Settings: Screen Time: Content &amp; Privacy Restrictions: Siri, or System Settings: Screen Time: Content &amp; Privacy: Siri. You can choose Siri Classic from the Siri menu, and it&#8217;s like you&#8217;re living in fall 2025 to summer 2026 again!<sup id="fnref-42133-siridisable"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/siri-classic-brings-old-things-back-to-life/#fn-42133-siridisable" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">3</a></sup> Siri Classic is almost identical—perhaps actually identical—to the version available in the version 26 operating systems.</p>
<p>If you choose to re-enable Siri AI, go to the same place and choose Siri AI (Beta). Go to Settings: Siri and re-enroll in the beta, too.</p>
<figure><img height="1150" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/remove-ai-features-27-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/remove-ai-features-27-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/remove-ai-features-27-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/remove-ai-features-27-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/remove-ai-features-27-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1380" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/remove-ai-features-27-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Two iOS Screen Time screenshots side-by-side showing Siri AI (Beta) parental controls, with settings for Broad World Knowledge, Writing Assistance (Don't Allow), Math Assistance, Image Creation, Realistic Images, Sensitive Topics, Explicit Language, and Extensions." /><figcaption>The Screen Time settings let you downgrade Siri AI and disable or downgrade certain Apple Intelligence features.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>That doesn&#8217;t seem very intelligent</h2>
<p>You can flip off—er, disable—other Apple Intelligence features, too:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Writing Assistance:</strong> So far, I&#8217;ve never found Writing Assistance (formerly Writing Tools) to be of any use. I keep Siri AI active, but disable this feature.</li>
<li><strong>Image Creation and Realistic Images:</strong> Disabling these options ensures that you don&#8217;t accidentally generate images, and that Siri doesn&#8217;t, either.</li>
<li><strong>Extensions:</strong> This option prevents ChatGPT from being consulted, whether pseudo-anonymously (without an account) or otherwise.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apple has other locations for turning off Apple Intelligence features scattered throughout Settings and apps. To exclude apps you don&#8217;t want to be used by Apple Intelligence for information it gathers across your system, go to Settings/System Settings: Siri: Excluded Apps, tap or click Add Apps, and pick apps. (Tap or click Edit to revise this list.)</p>
<p>Other places to make changes, either in Settings on iOS/iPadOS or as noted, are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Message summaries:</strong> Apps: Messages (iOS/iPadOS) or Messages: Settings: General (macOS), disable Summarize Messages.</li>
<li><strong>Email summaries:</strong> Apps: Mail (iOS/iPadOS) or Mail: Settings: Viewing (macOS), turn off Summarize Message Previews.</li>
<li><strong>Notification summaries:</strong> Notifications: Summarize Notifications, then disable Summarize Notifications.</li>
<li><strong>Priority notifications:</strong> Notifications: Prioritize Notifications (iOS/iPadOS), and turn off Prioritize Notifications.</li>
<li><strong>Personalized Smart Replies in Mail:</strong> Apps: Mail, then disable Personalize Smart Replies.</li>
<li><strong>Voicemail suggestions in Phone:</strong> Apps: Phone (iOS/iPadOS) or Phone: Settings: Calls (macOS), turn off Suggestions in Voicemail.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why is off so frightening?</h2>
<p>With a Siri that mostly delivers on what Apple promised a few years ago, you can see why the company wouldn&#8217;t want to have a simple &#8220;off&#8221; switch on Apple Intelligence features. Yet, if public opinion is any guide, some people don&#8217;t want to use LLMs in any substantive form.</p>
<p>This Siri AI downgrade is an option, but it feels like a half measure compared to a true off switch for Apple Intelligence, even if it were buried like the Siri Classic choice.</p>
